
  [1mThe fork:[0m is in-app the whole story, or is a notification an event that gets delivered through channels?

  - [1mIn-app only, permanently[0m — one [38;5;153mnotifications[39m row per user per event, with [38;5;153mread_at[39m. Buys: the simplest thing that
    works; no dispatch layer, no preferences, no retry logic, no bounce handling. Costs: users only find out when they
    open the app — which for "a task you care about changed" is often too late to matter. Adding a channel later means
    introducing the fan-out layer and migrating.
  - [1mIn-app now, email later[0m — notification event + per-recipient delivery records, one per channel. Buys: the fan-out
    seam exists from day one, so email is a new adapter rather than a new architecture; enables digesting/batching,
    which is the real fix for notification spam. Costs: more moving parts in the slice than in-app alone strictly needs;
    email brings deliverability, an outbound provider, and unsubscribe obligations.
  - [1mIn-app now, browser push later[0m — same fan-out shape, but the second channel is the Web Push API. Buys: real-time
    reach with no email provider or deliverability problem. Costs: a service worker, a permission prompt users decline,
    per-browser subscription endpoints that expire, and nothing at all for users who aren't on that browser.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m in-app now, email later — meaning we build the fan-out seam in the slice but implement exactly one
  adapter. The concrete slice cost is small (a [38;5;153mdeliveries[39m table and an adapter interface instead of a [38;5;153mread_at[39m column),
  and it buys the thing that actually makes notifications useful: reaching people who aren't looking at the app. Email
  also degrades gracefully in a way push doesn't — no permission prompt, no expiry.

  For transport within the in-app channel, I'd start with polling the unread count on an interval and leave SSE as a
  later swap behind the same client-side interface; real-time is a nice-to-have that costs connection management, and
  nothing in the slice needs sub-minute latency.
